Pattern A (what you did for Loans — simpler):
Abstract class only has variables, constructor, getters (no printX mentioned at all)
Subclass freely adds its own new printX() method, nobody forces it to

Pattern B (what this RoadAccidents example does):
Abstract class adds one extra line: public abstract void printAccidentReport();
This forces any subclass to implement printAccidentReport() — if a subclass forgets to write it, the code won't compile
Which should you use tomorrow?

Check the exact wording of the question. If it doesn't explicitly say the abstract class must declare the print method, either pattern will satisfy the rubric 
the marks are almost always awarded for "abstract class has constructor + variables + methods" without demanding this exact abstract-method technique.
Your Loan version (Pattern A) is simpler to write under time pressure and is what I'd recommend defaulting to,
unless a specific question phrase (like "the abstract class must also declare the print method, to be implemented by the subclass") tells you otherwise.
